# Claude Security -- suggested patch for F2: Multipart filename sanitized before HTML-unescape, allowing path separators to be reintroduced
# Applies to revision 78fea27f6900 (the revision the scan report describes).
#
# Verified by a panel of agents: an independent verifier reviewed this
# change against the finding, and a second, fresh reviewer re-challenged
# the bare diff for new vulnerabilities. The patch was written only
# because the panel stated all three of these with confidence:
#   - the change is highly targeted to this finding: every hunk is the sanitizer reorder/rejection in django/http/multipartparser.py or the tests for it in tests/file_uploads/tests.py; the test refactor to CANDIDATE_* lists is consumed by the three new tests; flake8 and isort clean
#   - the change introduces no new security vulnerability: sanitize_file_name output is always a suffix of the old pipeline's output and never contains '/' or '\'; no validation removed; probe confirmed bare drive prefixes (D:foo) were already reachable on the base via an encoded colon; the fresh adversarial reviewer of the bare diff found no exploitable regression and net attacker capability goes down
#   - beyond closing the finding, the change does not alter the code's behaviour or the inputs it accepts: file_uploads (37 tests) plus requests, test_client, test_client_regress, handlers, forms_tests.field_tests.test_filefield, file_storage, model_fields.test_filefield (476 tests) pass; the only newly turned-away inputs are '.' and '..' and their encoded/padded equivalents, which the finding names as the exploit input
#
# MultiPartParser.parse ran os.path.basename on the Content-Disposition filename before html.unescape, so an HTML-encoded separator was decoded into a real '/' afterwards and '.'/'..' were never rejected; the patch replaces IE_sanitize with sanitize_file_name, which decodes entities first, strips everything up to the last '/' and '\', keeps the existing whitespace strip, and returns None for '', '.', and '..' so the part is skipped, with the IE_sanitize name kept as an alias and the unused os import removed.
# Tests run: cd tests && python runtests.py file_uploads -v 2 (Ran 37 tests, OK); cd tests && python runtests.py requests test_client test_client_regress handlers forms_tests.field_tests.test_filefield file_storage model_fields.test_filefield -v 1 (Ran 476 tests, OK); same file_uploads tests against the unpatched base fail with AssertionError '../hax0rd.txt' != 'hax0rd.txt' and '.' is not None; flake8 and isort --check-only exit 0 (Python 3.9, asgiref 3.5.2)
#
# Apply, from the repository root:  git apply CLAUDE-SECURITY-20260904-161400/patches/F2.patch
#
